---
import Head from "../components/Head.astro";
import ZoomableImage from "../components/ZoomableImage.astro";
---

<script>
  // Function to handle highlighting
  function setupHighlighting() {
    // Get all elements with data-highlight attribute
    const highlightTriggers = document.querySelectorAll("[data-highlight]");

    // Add event listeners to each trigger element
    highlightTriggers.forEach((trigger) => {
      const highlightKey = trigger.getAttribute("data-highlight");

      // Find corresponding elements to highlight
      const highlightTargets = document.querySelectorAll(
        `[data-highlight-text="${highlightKey}"]`,
      );

      // Add mouseenter event listener
      trigger.addEventListener("mouseenter", () => {
        highlightTargets.forEach((target) => {
          target.classList.add("highlighted");
        });
      });

      // Add mouseleave event listener
      trigger.addEventListener("mouseleave", () => {
        highlightTargets.forEach((target) => {
          target.classList.remove("highlighted");
        });
      });
    });
  }

  // Run the setup when the DOM is fully loaded
  document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", setupHighlighting);
</script>
<style>
  main {
    font-family: "Open Sans Variable", sans-serif;
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 15px;
    color: #3a3a54;
    max-width: 450px;
    margin: 128px auto;
    padding: 0 16px;
  }

  h1 {
    text-align: center;
  }

  h3 {
    margin-bottom: 4px;
  }

  section {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }

  section + section {
    margin-top: 32px;
  }

  ul {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin-top: 4px;
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  li {
    opacity: 0.7;
    cursor: pointer;
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-style: dotted;
  }

  li + li {
    margin-top: 2px;
  }

  .project-title {
    display: flex;
    column-gap: 8px;
    align-items: center;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
  }

  .project-title h3 {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .project-link {
    text-decoration: none;
    color: #117be5;

    &:hover {
      text-decoration: underline;
    }
  }

  .project-info {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    row-gap: 8px;
  }

  .tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 4px;
  }

  .date {
    font-size: 13px;
    opacity: 0.6;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
  }

  .tag {
    flex-shrink: 0;
    border-radius: 6px;
    border: 1px solid #eee;
    padding: 2px 4px;
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .experience-header {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
  }

  .experiences-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    row-gap: 16px;
  }

  .reversed {
    flex-direction: column-reverse;
  }

  [data-hightlight] {
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  [data-highlight-text] {
    background-image: linear-gradient(to right, #ccf479 0%, #ccf479 100%);
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
    background-size: 0 100%;
    transition: background-size 0.8s ease-in-out;
  }

  .highlighted {
    background-size: 100% 100%;
  }

  .text-center {
    text-align: center;
  }
</style>

<Head title="Linear" />
<script async src="https://platform.twitter.com/widgets.js" charset="utf-8"
></script>
<main>
  <h1>Hello, Linear Team!</h1>
  <section>
    <h2>Why I want to join Linear</h2>
    <div class="text">
      <p>A week ago marked 4 years of happy usage of Linear.</p>
      <ZoomableImage
        width="100%"
        src="/images/linear-first-letter.png"
        alt="Linear first letter"
      />
      <p>
        I love the phrase Karri Saarinen wrote in the post «Why is quality so
        rare» - <em
          >«In a crowded market of issue tracking tools, Linear stood out
          through quality»</em
        >. This is exactly what I felt when I first opened Linear 4 years ago.
        It was obvious that every detail of the app was polished to perfection.
        One of the things that truly amazed me was the fact that you have to
        take into account the path that the user is leading their mouse through
        while navigating nested menus:
      </p>
      <blockquote class="twitter-tweet">
        <p lang="en" dir="ltr">
          Fun little detail that just landed in <a
            href="https://twitter.com/linear_app?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"
            >@linear_app</a
          >: <br /><br />When navigating in the contextual menu it’s now easier
          to move the mouse to sub-menus, since we paint out a little “safe
          area” between the cursor and the menu.<br /><br />(The area is
          highlighted in the clip.) <a href="https://t.co/HcMJyAuszA"
            >pic.twitter.com/HcMJyAuszA</a
          >
        </p>&mdash; Andreas Eldh (@eldh) <a
          href="https://twitter.com/eldh/status/1300485830302203910?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"
          >August 31, 2020</a
        >
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Until that tweet, I didn’t think about it. I didn’t know that you could
        care so much about little details here and there.
      </p>
      <p>
        After that, I decided to strive for the same level of attention when
        building things.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <script>
    function handleChangeSorting(event: any) {
      const selectedValue = event.target.value;
      const experiences = document.querySelector(".experiences-list")!;
      if (selectedValue === "first") {
        experiences.classList.toggle("reversed", false);
      } else {
        experiences.classList.toggle("reversed", true);
      }
    }

    const orderSelector = document.getElementById("order-selector")!;
    orderSelector.addEventListener("change", handleChangeSorting);
  </script>

  <section>
    <div class="experience-header">
      <h2>My experience</h2>
      <select id="order-selector">
        <option value="first">First - Last</option>
        <option value="last">Last - First</option>
      </select>
    </div>
    <div class="experiences-list">
      <div>
        <h3>Interactive Design Institute</h3>
        <div class="date">May 2016 - April 2017 (1 year)</div>
        <p>
          <strong>What I learned</strong>
          <ul>
            <li data-highlight="h1">
              To be the person who cares about the product the most
            </li>
            <li data-highlight="h2">To be proactive</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <div class="text">
          <p>
            One of my first jobs was the Junior Frontend Engineer position at
            Interactive Design Institute. My brightest memory of working at this
            place was the time I found out that one of our high traffic pages
            took 8 seconds to load. We had a lot of text on this page and
            despite the fact that we didn’t show it until user asks us to show
            it, we loaded it immediately at the page show. Everyone knew about
            this, but no one considered this a problem. I knew little at the
            time (I just started my career), but I already started to notice
            best practices on the other sites and apps. <span
              data-highlight-text="h2"
              >I asked for a permission to fix the problem and in an hour the
              solution that decreased load time to less than a second was ready.</span
            >
          </p>
          <p>
            <span data-highlight-text="h1">
              That it was the first time I realized that I shouldn’t rely on
              other people, I should be the one who cares.
            </span>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3>Avito</h3>
        <div class="date">October 2017 - December 2018 (1 year 3 months)</div>
        <strong>What I learned</strong>
        <ul>
          <li data-highlight="h3">Understand other peoples code</li>
          <li data-highlight="h4">To see a product from user's perspective</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="text">
          <p>
            In this company, I started to refactor old code from jQuery to
            React.
            <span data-highlight-text="h3"
              >That task taught me to understand other people’s code and their
              intentions</span
            >. Later, we started to build a new feature - improved Value Added
            Services, which significantly boosted our conversion. <span
              data-highlight-text="h4"
              >I participated in UX demos with real users to better understand
              the problems they encounter. Until this demo, I had never tried to
              look at my apps through the eyes of the users. I never realized
              that things that are obvious to you might be a mystery to other
              people</span
            >. For example, we had an important text that users were supposed to
            read, but according to the eye-tracking device that we used, no one
            even saw it!
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3>RedMadRobot</h3>
        <div class="date">May 2019 - July 2024 (5 years)</div>
        <strong>What I learned</strong>
        <ul>
          <li data-highlight="h5">
            Think about the future while building products
          </li>
          <li data-highlight="h6">Optimize own workflows</li>
          <li data-highlight="h7">Learn new things not only at work</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="text">
          <p>
            This is an outsourcing company, so, as you can imagine, we were
            building a number of products instead of concentrating on one or
            two. Some people consider this not useful experience, but I’m so
            grateful to this company for teaching me almost everything I know to
            this moment. <span data-highlight-text="h5"
              >I learned how to build products from scratch and to architect
              them in a way that won’t hurt in the future</span
            >. <span data-highlight-text="h6"
              >I also realized that you need to optimize the developer
              experience too to deliver a better result. For example, when you
              start a new product twice a year, you need to shorten the time it
              takes to create a new project. I’ve built a solution for that - a
              CLI instrument that creates a project from template and allows to
              add new files based on our company guidelines</span
            >. <span data-highlight-text="h7"
              >In this company, I also came to the realization that you have to
              move twice as fast as you can to get somewhere. I started to read
              more, watch more, and build more in my spare time</span
            >.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3>EasyBiz</h3>
        <div class="date">September 2024 - July 2025 (11 months)</div>
        <strong>What I learned</strong>
        <ul>
          <li data-highlight="h8">To improve things in order to stand out</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="text">
          <p>
            We were a small startup with a lot of competitors. <span
              data-highlight-text="h8"
              >In situation like this you really need to stand out to make an
              impression. I refactored some code to make our app faster and more
              pleasant to use. For example, I’ve rebuilt our table component
              from the scratch. It became twice as fast, the loading time became
              so small, the end user didn’t even have time to notice loading
              skeletons</span
            >.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>My personal projects</h2>
    </div>
    <section>
      <div>
        <div class="project-title">
          <img src="/icons/kona.svg" alt="Kona logo" width="32" height="32" />
          <a class="project-link" href="https://kona.to" target="_blank">
            <h3>Kona</h3>
